Doom for Inferno — completed milestone log (historical)
=======================================================

This file preserves the detailed close-out notes for milestones 1-7
as they were completed during development.  The forward-looking
plan (status, missing-feature milestones, process rules, porting
notes) lives in PLAN.

Milestones (each independently testable)
----------------------------------------
  [x] 1  WAD loader + directory dump + lump read     (doom -d)
  [x] 2  video path: 320x200 framebuffer blitted onto the emu
         screen via writepixels; measure fps
         (doom -t: xor-plasma, ~101 fps at 320x200 XRGB32;
         screen is win-x11a's forced 32bpp, so the framebuffer
         is XRGB32 BGRX, not RGB24 — RGB24 writes fail with
         "loadimage: insufficient data")
  [x] 3  renderer: COLORMAP + PLAYPAL, flats, walls
         (doom -r: vanilla R_DrawColumn/R_DrawSpan, eager
         texture compositing from patches, 49 fps full-screen
         STARTAN3 wall + FLOOR4_8 floor with light gradients)
   [x] 4  map load: LINEDEFS/SECTORS/SIDEDEFS/VERTEXES,
          geometry structs as Limbo adts
          (doom -m: map.m/map.b Level adt, shared ref Sectors so
          movers can mutate heights; E1M1 checks all match vanilla:
          467 v / 475 l / 648 s / 85 sec / 138 things, two-sided
          173, player1 1056,-3616 angle 90)
  [x] 5  game tics: player movement, mobj state tables,
         Doom's tick loop (35Hz) driven from Sys->millisec
         (doom -g: gametest runs 105 tics at exactly 35 tics/sec;
         spawn 1056,-3616 angle 90 health 100 state 149 S_PLAY;
         95 mobjs / 6 kills / 37 items on E1M1 skill medium;
         brute-force collision over 475 lines + all mobjs;
         geninfo.py generates info.m/info.b from info.c/info.h;
         fixed bare-identifier enum bug: Limbo merges consecutive
         `A, B, C,` lists into one declaration ending at the next
         `: con value` — all SPR_/S_/MT_ constants evaluated to 1
         until geninfo.py was fixed to emit `: con iota;`)
  [x] 6  input: /dev/scancode raw keycodes -> event chan
         (rewritten: /dev/keyboard had no key-up, relied on X
         auto-repeat + a hold-decay hack — stuttered between
         repeats, leaked movement after release, felt buffered)
         - kbdreader opens /dev/scancode: raw X11 keycodes,
           press=code, release=code|0x80 (emu win-x11a.c xkeyboard
           gkscanq path; opening /dev/scancode routes ALL key
           events there — /dev/keyboard goes silent)
         - keysym() maps evdev+8 keycodes (Esc=9, W=25, A=38,
           S=39, D=40, Space=65, arrows=111/113/114/116,
           LCtrl=37, RCtrl=105) to keystate indices; unknown
           codes ignored
         - keystate is 0/1 (true down/up), NO decay
         - main loop: key events ONLY update keystate and
           `continue`; tics come SOLELY from the tick timer —
           key-driven tics made the game surge at auto-repeat
           rate and stutter between repeats (the "not smooth"
           bug); key-to-motion latency = one tic max (28ms)
         - keyboard.m is header-only: define Esc/Up/Down/Left/
           Right/LCtrl/RCtrl constants inline in doom.b
         - floor renders correctly via drawspan + convert + present
   [x] 7  world view: BSP walk -> seg clip -> wall columns DONE;
          floor/ceiling visplanes DONE (r_plane.c port)
          viewx/viewy/viewz/viewangle from console player;
          occlusion array (solid[] / ceilingclip/floorclip);
          projection: screenx = centerx - left*proj/depth;
          depth = dx*cos+dy*sin, left = dy*cos-dx*sin;
          view.m/view.b module; walls render textured (320 cols/frame);
          two-sided lines: upper/lower textures + portal occlusion
          with clamping; one-sided: midtexture columns
          visplanes: findplane/markplane/drawplanes + mapplane;
          per-column [top,bottom] mark, span-run accumulator -> mapplane;
          yslope[y]=proj/|dy| (16.16), distscale[x]=1/cos;
           BUG FIX: basexscale/baseyscale must be vsin/vcos DIVIDED by
           proj (vanilla folds /centerxfrac into them).  bare vsin/vcos
           made ds_xstep ~160x too large -> texture aliased to a solid
           color (the flat-blue floor); added fixeddiv to rend.
            BUG FIX (SUPERSEDED by occlusion rework below): markplane
            unioned each column's strips into one [top,bottom] range, so a
            farther plane spanned gaps that nearer planes owned -> lower
            blue floor painted over higher gray floor, seams not flush.
            Split into markfloor/markceil with shared per-column
            floorline[x] (init h) / ceilline[x] (init -1).  This bounded
            each plane correctly but broke on cross-plane interactions
            (vanilla shares ONE clip pair between planes and walls); the
            floorline/ceilline system was fully replaced - see the
            vanilla-clip occlusion rework entry below.
            RESOLVED (user-confirmed): floor/ceiling rotation shear is
            gone.  Final root causes, found by deriving vanilla geometry
            end-to-end and simulating the Limbo logic numerically:
            1. buildfsine FREQUENCY/PHASE: real Doom is
               finesine[k]=round(sin((k+0.5)*PI/4096)*65536) (full circle
               at 8192, half-step phase; verified vs tables.c, max err 1).
               The port generated sin(k*PI/2048) - DOUBLE frequency, no
               phase, and a Taylor series truncated at x^7 (0.23 rad error
               near pi).  Every origin lookup was garbage regardless of
               sign/algebra fixes.
            2. xtofine BINARY SEARCH: compared sn*proj vs tds*cs (scale
               mismatch, rhs ~409x lhs) so the search returned the
               +/-1024 boundary for nearly every column; and stored +delta
               where vanilla xtoviewangle is NEGATIVE for x>centerx (ray
               angle = viewangle - delta), mirroring the origin against
               the step.  Fixed to sn<<16 vs tds*cs and (-lo)&FINEMASK.
            3. distscale OVERFLOW: isqrt(sum<<16) overflowed int32 for
               sum>32767 (|tan|>0.63, outer third of each screen side),
               falling back to 1.0 while the origin angle still rotated.
               Rebuilt distscale from xtofine like vanilla (r_main.c:734:
               FixedDiv(FRACUNIT, abs(finecosine[xtoviewangle>>19]))),
               which also ties length to the same quantized column angle
               as the origin.
            4. yslope half-pixel: vanilla dy=abs(((i-h/2)<<16)+32768);
               the +32768 bias was dropped.  yslope=(proj<<32)/dy now.
            (baseyscale sign fix from the prior pass was correct
            algebra (+vcos/(proj<<16)) but was not the shear cause.)
            REPEAT-MISTAKE NOTE: individual pieces were "verified" in
            isolation across several passes while shear persisted.  What
            finally worked: derive the FULL pipeline (table formula ->
            column angle -> origin -> step) from the raw vanilla source
            and compare against exact geometry numerically before
             touching code.  Never trust piecewise verification.
           RESOLVED (model-verified, user validation pending): floor/
           ceiling occlusion rework - replaced floorline/ceilline with
           vanilla's shared-clip model.  Full pipeline derived from raw
           r_segs.c (R_RenderSegLoop/R_StoreWallRange), r_plane.c
           (R_CheckPlane), r_bsp.c (R_Subsector), then encoded as a
           Python state machine (/tmp/opencode/occ_model.py, 11 scenarios
           incl. portals/steps/sky/gaps): port_fix claims + final open
           sets + drawn rows EXACTLY equal vanilla on every scenario,
           while port_now fails exactly the predicted bugs.  Root causes
           fixed: (1) back-sector plane claims at portals double-claimed
           flat portals / painted back floors over front floors at
           steps - vanilla claims back planes only via the NEXT seg;
           (2) markfloor bottom off-by-one (was inclusive fclip, vanilla
           is floorclip-1); (3) no mutual clip clamps on two-sided strip
           marks; (4) floorline/ceilline cross-plane misbehavior (far
           floor discarded when t>b -> holes; ceilline overlap row);
           (5) no viewz/sky gate on plane choice -> garbage claims when
           floor is above the eye (vanilla: floorplane only below eye,
           ceilingplane only above eye or sky); (6) untextured upper "-"
           advanced the clip past undrawn rows -> black band (advance
           only to the front ceiling edge); (7) one-sided continue-before-
           marks skipped claims+close on degenerate walls; (8) min/max
           union fused disjoint strips across gaps (A-B-A ceilings) ->
           now per-seg R_CheckPlane spawns a duplicate when the seg's
           range already contains a claim, so each column row has exactly
           one owner and draw order is irrelevant.  solid[] now stores
           vanilla ceilingclip+1/floorclip (first-open/one-past-last);
           one-sided walls close solid[2x]=h, solid[2x+1]=-1; dead
           floorline/ceilline/markz/marklight/markflat removed.
           NOTE for future sprite work: sprites will need vanilla
           sprtopclip semantics - solid[] no longer holds wall edges.
           BUG FIX (left-edge wall corruption): the per-column frac used
           for scale/z/u interpolation was computed in int32 as
           ((x-ox1)<<16)/tspan, with a comment assuming |x-ox1|<=320.
           But ox1 is the ORIGINAL projected endpoint, kept unclamped as
           the interpolation basis - a near-plane-clipped endpoint
           projects to |x| ~ 100k px, so for segs extending off the
           LEFT screen edge (x-ox1)~64000 and <<16 overflows int32.
           Right-extending segs never overflow (ox1 stays on-screen ->
           x-ox1 <= 320), which is why only the leftmost side broke.
           Garbage frac (negative or far-off positive, position/angle
           dependent) extrapolates scale/id: magnified ("zoomed") wall
           texture, or scale/id <= 0 -> column skipped ("textures not
           rendering").  Verified numerically: ox1=-64000 gives frac
           -0.024 where truth is 0.995, on every column.  Now computed
           in big (result <= 65536 since x <= ox2) in both addline and
           drawtwosided column loops.
          sky (F_SKY1) DONE (user validation pending): derived from
          vanilla r_plane.c R_DrawPlanes:390, r_sky.c/h, r_segs.c:525,
          r_main.c:719.  Sky is NOT a flat - it is drawn as wall
          texture columns from the SKY1 TEXTURE (shareware episode 1;
          g_game.c maps episodes to SKY1/SKY2/SKY3), fullbright
          (colormap row 0), iscale = pspriteiscale = 1.0 (one texel
          per screen pixel), texturemid = 100*FRACUNIT (skytexturemid)
          so texture row = screen row when centery=100, wrapping every
          tex.height rows; column = (viewangle+xtoviewangle[x])>>22
          (ANGLETOSKYSHIFT) = ((vangle>>19)+xtofine[x])>>3 in fine
          units, wrapped by tex.width - the sky turns with the view,
          no parallax.  Also: findplane normalizes sky to z=0/light=0
          (vanilla R_FindPlane:218 - all skys map together, seamless
          at sky-sky portals), and the vanilla sky hack (r_segs.c:525):
          when both ceilings are sky the upper texture collapses to
          zero height and is never drawn (outdoor height changes flow
          as continuous sky; our undrawn-upper clip path already
          matches vanilla's markceiling branch).  Numerically checked:
          vertical mapping exact; column matches vanilla everywhere
          except <=1 texture column at quantization boundaries near
          the wrap (both quantize to 1/1024 turn; fine-unit phase
          differs by <1/8 column - invisible, seamless via wrap).
           F_SKY1 marker flat stays excluded from rflat (rend.b:99);
           drawplanes special-cases it by name before the flat lookup.
            lighting (outdoor pitch-black fix) DONE (user-validated):
            the port computed light = (sec.lightlevel*31)/255
           and passed it straight through as the COLORMAP ROW
           (drawcolumn/drawspan: cm = row*256) - INVERTED vs vanilla.
           COLORMAP row 0 = fullbright, row 31 = darkest; E1M1 outdoor
           sectors are lightlevel 255/224 -> old rows 31/27 = pitch
           black, and the old curves cross at 128 (old row 15), which
           is exactly why interiors looked plausible and only the
           outside went black.  Parsed doom1.wad (sectors lump 26-byte,
           name-based): 85 sectors, 7 sky sectors, outdoor lights
           255 x6 + 224 x1, indoor histogram 128..255.  Full vanilla
           model now implemented (r_main.h constants, R_InitLightTables
           r_main.c:610, scalelight build in R_SetViewSize r_main.c:741,
           R_MapPlane r_plane.c:160, R_StoreWallRange r_segs.c:649):
           light index i = lightlevel>>LIGHTSEGSHIFT (extralight=0, no
           light-amp pickups) picks 1 of 16 bands; startmap = (15-i)*4;
           zlight[i][j] (j = plane distance>>LIGHTZSHIFT, clamp
           MAXLIGHTZ-1=127) = clamp(startmap - scale/2) with scale =
           FixedDiv((w/2)*FRACUNIT, (j+1)<<LIGHTZSHIFT)>>LIGHTSCALESHIFT
           (= 160/(j+1) at w=320); scalelight[i][j] (j =
           wall scale>>LIGHTSCALESHIFT, clamp MAXLIGHTSCALE-1=47) =
           clamp(startmap - j*320/w/DISTMAP) (= startmap - j/2).
           Wall lightnum gets the orientation hack (r_segs.c:649-653:
           horizontal segs -1, vertical +1, clamp [0,15]).  Visplane
           light field now stores the light INDEX (claims pass pllight);
           mapplane picks the row per distance band; addline and
           drawtwosided column draws pick it per scale band (scalelight
           was built in view.b init alongside the other tables).
            Numeric check BEFORE editing: lightlevel 255 -> row 0 at
            every band (outdoors fullbright), 192 -> 0 near .. 12 far,
            128 -> 0 near .. 28 far (vanilla distance darkening), walls
            row 0 at scale index 47 (near, bright) .. startmap far.
            P_SlideMove (wall slide + flush-to-wall) DONE (user
            validation pending): the port zeroed momentum on a blocked
            move (milestone 5 stub) instead of vanilla's flush + along-
            wall redirect, so the player stopped short of walls and
            could not slide.  Root cause: every other collision piece
            (pointonlineside, boxonlineside, pitcheckline,
            pitcheckthing, checkposition, trymove step checks,
            xymovement, map slopetype/bbox, tmbbox conventions) was
            already byte-equivalent to vanilla; only the slide pipeline
            was missing.  Full transcription now in game.b from raw
            sources: P_SlideMove + P_HitSlideLine + PTR_SlideTraverse
            (p_map.c:622-830), P_InterceptVector + P_PointOnDivlineSide
            + PIT_AddLineIntercepts crossing test + P_TraverseIntercepts
            maxfrac=FRACUNIT stop (p_maputl.c), R_PointToAngle octant
            body (r_main.c:262), SlopeDiv (tables.c), FixedDiv
            saturation (m_fixed.c), and the EXACT vanilla tantoangle
            [2049] table embedded in init() (extracted from tables.c,
            not recomputed from atan).  No blockmap: the three leading-
            corner rays brute-force ALL lines with vanilla's crossing
            test and frac in [0,FRACUNIT] - order-independent vs the
            min-frac scan (stops at first blocker), vanilla's blockmap
            blocks only prefilter candidates.  Signed/unsigned edges:
            deltaangle > ANG180 as s32 (negative but != ANG180), u32
            >>19 & FINEMASK = logical shift, SlopeDiv unsigned (num<<3)
            wrap done in big, saturating FixedDiv before rend's raising
            div (rend->fixeddiv never touched).  Numeric checks BEFORE
            editing (/tmp/opencode/slide_check.py): SlopeDiv port ==
            vanilla 3012 cases; pointtoangle2 worst 0.635 fine steps
            vs exact atan2 (vanilla's own table quantization);
            hitslide direction residual <=2% of the original normal
            component, length inside the AproxDistance 6% envelope;
            intercept frac divergences only at crossing_sin ~2e-5
            (near-parallel) where vanilla's FixedMul(>>8) truncation
            is identical and P_TraverseIntercepts drops the frac
            anyway; flush sim: every -0x800 fudge stays clear of the
            wall AABB.  Startup smoke: emu timeout 8s, E1M1 t=70, no
            errors, no process left running.
